          ["16. Distinguish chemical reactions and nuclear reactions", "Chemical reactions involve rearrangement of electrons; nuclear reactions involve changes in the nucleus.\nChemical reactions do not change the identity of the element; nuclear reactions may convert one element into another.\nChemical reactions involve relatively small energy changes; nuclear reactions involve very large energy changes.\nChemical reaction rates are affected by temperature, pressure and catalysts; nuclear reactions are generally not affected by ordinary chemical conditions.\nChemical reactions usually involve valence electrons; nuclear reactions involve protons and neutrons.\nMass change is negligible in ordinary chemical reactions but measurable in nuclear reactions.\nExample: combustion of carbon is chemical; radioactive decay is nuclear."],
          ["17. Differences between fats and oils", "Fats are generally solid at room temperature; oils are generally liquid.\nFats contain a higher proportion of saturated fatty acids; oils usually contain more unsaturated fatty acids.\nFats are commonly obtained from animal sources; oils are commonly obtained from plant or fish sources.\nFats usually have higher melting points; oils have lower melting points.\nBoth are triglycerides formed from glycerol and fatty acids.\nBoth serve as energy stores and provide insulation in living organisms."],
          ["18. Greener synthesis for adipic acid and alcohol", "Green synthesis aims to reduce hazardous reagents, waste and energy use.\nAdipic acid can be produced by greener oxidation routes using hydrogen peroxide with suitable catalysts, reducing reliance on nitric acid and limiting nitrous oxide emissions.\nUse safer solvents or water where practical and optimize temperature and pressure.\nAlcohols can be prepared through catalytic hydration of alkenes or fermentation of sugars, depending on the alcohol required.\nFermentation uses renewable feedstocks and can operate under relatively mild conditions.\nImprove atom economy, catalyst reuse and product separation to reduce waste.\nExact routes depend on the specific alcohol intended by the question."],
          ["19. Describe photosensitization", "Photosensitization occurs when a substance called a sensitizer absorbs light and transfers energy or an electron to another molecule.\nThe sensitizer absorbs radiation and enters an excited state.\nIt transfers energy or an electron to the acceptor molecule.\nThe acceptor then undergoes a chemical reaction.\nThe sensitizer may return to its original state and be reused.\nExamples include dye-sensitized reactions and chlorophyll-assisted energy transfer in photosynthesis.\nApplications include photochemical synthesis, photography and some biological processes."],
          ["20. Thermoplastics and thermosetting plastics", "Thermoplastics soften on heating and harden on cooling repeatedly; thermosetting plastics set permanently after curing.\nThermoplastics have mostly linear or branched chains; thermosets have a highly cross-linked network.\nThermoplastics can usually be remoulded and recycled by melting; thermosets cannot be remelted into a new shape.\nThermoplastics are often more flexible; thermosets are generally hard and rigid.\nExamples of thermoplastics: PVC, PET and polyethylene.\nExamples of thermosets: Bakelite and melamine-formaldehyde resin.\nThermoplastics are used in packaging and pipes; thermosets are used in electrical fittings and heat-resistant components."],
          ["21. Soddy’s group displacement law", "During alpha decay, the atomic number decreases by 2 and the mass number decreases by 4; the element moves two places backward in the periodic table.\nDuring beta-minus decay, the atomic number increases by 1 while the mass number remains unchanged; the element moves one place forward.\nGamma emission does not change the atomic number or mass number.\nThese changes help identify the daughter nuclide formed during radioactive decay.\nExample of alpha decay: ²³⁸₉₂U → ²³⁴₉₀Th + ⁴₂He.\nExample of beta-minus decay: ¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + β⁻.\nThe law is useful for tracing radioactive transformations."],
          ["22. Iodine value, Reichert–Meissl value and saponification value of oil", "Iodine value: grams of iodine absorbed by 100 g of fat or oil; indicates degree of unsaturation.\nHigher iodine value generally indicates more carbon–carbon double bonds.\nReichert–Meissl (RM) value: millilitres of 0.1 N alkali needed to neutralize volatile, water-soluble fatty acids distilled from 5 g of fat.\nRM value helps distinguish fats such as butterfat from many other fats.\nSaponification value: milligrams of KOH required to saponify 1 g of fat or oil.\nHigher saponification value generally indicates shorter average fatty-acid chain length.\nThese values are used for characterization, quality control and detection of adulteration."],
          ["23. Explain Jablonski diagram", "A Jablonski diagram shows electronic energy states and transitions during light absorption and emission.\nGround singlet state: S₀.\nAbsorption raises a molecule from S₀ to an excited singlet state such as S₁ or S₂.\nVibrational relaxation and internal conversion release energy without light emission.\nFluorescence is emission from an excited singlet state to S₀ and is usually rapid.\nIntersystem crossing may transfer the molecule from a singlet state to a triplet state (T₁).\nPhosphorescence is emission from T₁ to S₀ and is generally slower than fluorescence."]
