            "16. Explain the theme of “The Ballad of Father Gilligan” and assess the father figure portrayed by Yeats.",
            "W. B. Yeats’s “The Ballad of Father Gilligan” portrays a priest whose compassion is limited by physical exhaustion.\nFather Gilligan serves a community where illness and death repeatedly call upon him, and he becomes so tired that he falls asleep after a widow seeks his help.\nHe later learns that the dying man believed a messenger from heaven had visited him.\nHis initial guilt gives way to gratitude and awe as he recognizes divine mercy.\nThe poem presents the priest as a human father figure: caring and dutiful, yet vulnerable to fatigue and error.\nHis grief is important because it shows that he takes his responsibility seriously.\nAt the same time, the outcome offers comfort by suggesting that grace does not depend entirely on human perfection.\nYeats’s ballad form and narrative movement make the story accessible while building its emotional and religious effect.\nThe poem combines sympathy for a weary servant of the community with faith in a compassionate power beyond human limits."
