            "17. Analyse the idea of a “happy life” in the poem A Happy Life.",
            "The poem “A Happy Life” invites readers to reconsider the meaning of happiness.\nRather than presenting happiness as wealth, status or constant excitement, it draws attention to simplicity, peace and a close relationship with the natural world.\nThe encounter between the poet and the lamb offers a moment of reflection on innocence and the value of a life free from unnecessary conflict.\nNature provides an alternative to the pressures and greed of human society.\nThe speaker’s response suggests that happiness can be found by noticing ordinary experiences and valuing contentment over endless acquisition.\nThis does not mean that every difficulty can be avoided, but that people can choose attitudes and habits that protect inner peace.\nThe poem also encourages respect for living creatures and the environments they inhabit.\nIts vision of happiness is therefore ethical as well as personal: a good life involves simplicity, gentleness and an awareness of the world beyond oneself.\nSpecific images from the prescribed poem should be added to support these points."
